📌  Kubernetes: Node in NotReady state

🔴 What Does Node NotReady Mean?
A node is NotReady when the kubelet is not reporting healthy status to the control plane.
Pods on this node may stop working or get evicted.


In Kubernetes, a Node is marked as NotReady when the control plane determines that the node is unhealthy or unreachable.
Here’s the clear breakdown.

When is a Node marked NotReady?
A Node becomes NotReady when the kubelet on that node stops reporting healthy status to the control plane, or reports unhealthy conditions.


• Kubelet down 
• Container runtime down 
• Network broken (API server unreachable) 
• Disk full 
• CNI plugin broken

1️⃣ Check Node Status & Conditions

Look at Conditions:
	• Ready = False
	• NetworkUnavailable
	• MemoryPressure
	• DiskPressure
	• PIDPressure


2️⃣ Check Kubelet Status (MOST COMMON CAUSE
If kubelet is down or unhealthy, node becomes NotReady.
Common reasons:
	• Kubelet crashed
	• Cert expired
	• Wrong config
	• Node rebooted and kubelet didn’t restart

3️⃣ Check Node Connectivity to Control Plane

The node must reach the API server.
Check:
	• Network connectivity
	• Firewall rules
	• Security groups (cloud)

curl https://<api-server-ip>:6443

4️⃣ Container Runtime Issues
If container runtime fails, kubelet cannot manage pods.

systemctl status containerd
# or
systemctl status docker

Common problems:

Runtime crashed

Disk full

Socket permission issues


5️⃣ Disk, Memory, and CPU Pressure

Resource pressure can mark node NotReady.


df -h
free -m
top

If:
	• Disk full → DiskPressure
	• Low memory → MemoryPressure

8️⃣ Certificates Expiry
Expired kubelet certs cause silent failures.
Check:

journalctl -u kubelet | grep certificate
Fix:

kubeadm certs renew
systemctl restart kubelet

