📌  If kubelet stops working on a worker node, will the pod on the node run ?
The short answer is yes, the pod will continue to run, but the Kubernetes control plane will lose the ability to manage it. 
Since the kubelet is the agent responsible for communicating between the master node and the worker node, its failure creates a "blind spot" in your cluster. 

LinkedIn +1
What Happens Immediately
• The Pod Keeps Running: The actual containers are managed by the container runtime (like containerd or Docker), not the kubelet itself. If the runtime is still healthy, the processes inside your pod will keep executing.
• Node Status becomes NotReady: After a short grace period (defaulting to 40 seconds), the master node will mark that worker node as NotReady or Unknown because it has stopped receiving "heartbeat" updates from the kubelet. 

Stack Overflow +3
What Happens After a Few Minutes
• Pod Eviction: By default, if a node remains NotReady for 5 minutes, the control plane will mark the pods on that node for deletion.
• Rescheduling: If your pods are managed by a Deployment or ReplicaSet, the control plane will automatically start new copies of those pods on other healthy worker nodes to maintain your desired replica count.
• The "Zombie" Pod: On the original node, the pod continues to run in reality, but because the kubelet is down, the API server cannot send a "delete" command to actually stop it. It effectively becomes a "zombie" pod that is still consuming local resources but is no longer part of the active cluster state. 


🔑 Interview-Ready Summary
	A node is NotReady when kubelet can’t communicate with the control plane.
	I check node conditions, kubelet status, container runtime, network/CNI, resource pressure, and API server connectivity.

📌  What is the Difference b/n HPA and VPA ? 
HPA – Horizontal Pod Autoscaler
What it does:
Scales the number of pod replicas up or down.
Scales:
➡️ Out (add pods) and In (remove pods)
Based on:
	• CPU utilization
	• Memory utilization
	• Custom metrics (e.g., requests per second)

VPA – Vertical Pod Autoscaler
What it does:
Scales the resources (CPU/memory) of individual pods.
Scales:
⬆️ Up (more CPU/memory) and ⬇️ Down (less CPU/memory)
Based on:
	• Actual resource usage over time
How it behaves:
	• Recommends or applies better CPU/memory requests & limits
	• Often restarts pods to apply changes
Modes:
	• Off – Only recommendations
	• Initial – Set resources at pod creation
	• Auto – Adjust resources and restart pods


How do we spinup nodes in kubernetes when pods are unscheduleable/pending due to resource issue even though HPA is enabled 

Using Cluster autoscaler or Karpenter.


Why HPA alone does NOT fix Pending pods
HPA (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler):
	• Watches metrics (CPU, memory, custom metrics)
	• Increases/decreases replica count
	• Assumes the cluster already has capacity
If:
	• Pods are Pending
	• Reason = Insufficient cpu / Insufficient memory
👉 HPA may increase replicas, but no nodes are added, so pods stay Pending forever.

What actually spins up nodes: Cluster Autoscaler
To automatically add nodes when pods can’t be scheduled, you need:
✅ Cluster Autoscaler
It:
	• Detects unschedulable pods
	• Increases node group size
	• Removes nodes when underutilized
How to enable node autoscaling (by environment)
1️⃣ Managed Kubernetes (Recommended)
EKS (AWS)
	• Use Cluster Autoscaler or Karpenter
	• Requires:
		○ Auto Scaling Groups (ASG)
		○ IAM permissions
	• Preferred modern option: Karpenter
GKE (Google Cloud)
	• Enable Node Pool Autoscaling
	• Cluster Autoscaler is built-in
AKS (Azure)
	• Enable Cluster Autoscaler
	• Configure min/max node count



✅ Explain the workflow - 


Here’s the simple, step-by-step flow of what happens when you run:

kubectl create deploy mydeploy --image=nginx


Think of it as “kubectl → API server → controllers → nodes”.

1. You run the command (kubectl)

kubectl is just a client tool

It does not create containers itself

It reads your kubeconfig and talks to the cluster

👉 kubectl sends a request to the Kubernetes API Server

2. API Server receives the request

The API Server:

Authenticates you (who are you?)

Authorizes you (are you allowed?)

Validates the request

It converts the command into a Deployment object like:

Name: mydeploy

Image: nginx

Replicas: 1 (default)

👉 The Deployment is stored in etcd (cluster database)

3. Deployment Controller wakes up

Kubernetes has many controllers running in the control plane

The Deployment Controller notices:

“A new Deployment exists, but no Pods are running”

👉 It creates a ReplicaSet

4. ReplicaSet Controller creates Pods

The ReplicaSet says:

“I must ensure 1 Pod is running”

The ReplicaSet Controller:

Creates a Pod object

👉 The Pod is saved in etcd (still not running yet)

5. Scheduler assigns the Pod to a node

The Scheduler sees:

“This Pod has no node assigned”

It:

Checks resource availability

Applies rules, constraints, taints/tolerations

Picks the best node

👉 Pod is assigned to a node (e.g., worker-node-1)

6. Kubelet on the node starts the container

On worker-node-1, the kubelet notices a new Pod assigned to it

Kubelet:

Pulls the nginx image

Creates the container using the container runtime (containerd / CRI-O)

Starts the container

👉 NGINX is now running 🎉

7. Status is updated

Kubelet reports back:

Pod is Running

Container is healthy

API Server updates the Pod status

👉 kubectl get pods now shows a running Pod


