✅   In Kubernetes, kubectl cordon and kubectl drain are both used to manage nodes, but they do different things and are often used together during maintenance.

kubectl cordon <node>
What it does
	• Marks the node as unschedulable
	• No new Pods will be scheduled on the node
	• Existing Pods stay running
kubectl drain <node>
What it does
	1. Cordon the node automatically
	2. Evict Pods from the node
	3. Reschedule Pods onto other nodes (if possible)

✅ Explain PDB 
In Kubernetes, PDB stands for Pod Disruption Budget.
It is a policy object that protects your application’s availability during voluntary disruptions by controlling how many pods can be unavailable at the same time.


2. What a PDB actually does
A Pod Disruption Budget:
	• Specifies minimum number of pods that must remain available
OR
	• Specifies maximum number of pods that can be unavailable
Kubernetes will block pod evictions if violating the PDB.
1. Why Pod Disruption Budget exists
Kubernetes sometimes needs to intentionally stop pods, for example:
	• Node maintenance / OS upgrades
	• Cluster upgrades
	• Draining a node
	• Scaling down nodes
	• Rolling updates
These are called voluntary disruptions
